  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nIssued at 125 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nTrough over the eastern CONUS continues to break down through \nTuesday/Wednesday, with the ridge that has been locked in place \noverhead tracking easterly. Upper level flow will then increase out \nof the southwest as a deep trof digs into the Desert Southwest. A \nstrong fetch of moisture in connection with the Eastern Pacific will \nset up, directing an unseasonably moist airmass into the central \nCONUS. This increase in moisture is aided by tropical systems that \nwill get caught in the flow from the Eastern Pacific, serving as a \nmoisture conveyor into the region, with plenty of forcing to favor \nprecipitation chances. Not only will this help supply moisture to \nthe region, but llvl southerly/southwesterly flow will usher Gulf \nmoisture in as well. Thus, an anomalously moist airmass will be in \nplace through mid week, with PWATS between 1.50-2.00\" or higher \nmoving in. As such, the WPC has highlighted our area in a Slight \nRisk, level 2 of 4, for Excessive Rainfall and Flash Flooding \nthrough mid week.\n\nThe wave digging into the Desert Southwest will slowly traverse over \nthe Rockies, guiding bouts of energy towards the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley, with a baroclinic zone setting up nearby. This \nbaroclinic zone will serve as a focus for the potential for \nrounds of showers/storms, with heavy rainfall possible. There \nremains uncertainty on where this zone of heavier precipitation \nwill set up, but we have noted a northwesterly trend in this \nrainfall axis over the last 24 hours. This trend has shifted the\naxis of heaviest precipitation out of our area at this time, \nbut keeps it close to our northwest. Along with the shift in the\nheavy rainfall axis, the timing for us has also trended later \nover recent forecast runs. We were initially looking at the \npotential first round of showers/storms moving in Tuesday night,\nbut we are now looking at potential onset closer to the \nWednesday AM timeframe. These precipitation chances will largely\nremain roughly along and north of a line from Sigourney to \nDubuque, Iowa, with the better and more widespread rainfall \nchances moving in Wednesday night through Thursday (60-80%\nchances). Through this two-day stretch, WPC continues to favor \na widespread 1.00-2.00\" of rain for our area, with locally \nhigher amounts possible in eastern Iowa. There remains \nuncertainty in these amounts, as ensemble guidance generally \nfavors 1.00\" or more of rain with about 30-50% probabilities. \nProbabilities are higher with the Euro suite, but they remain an\noutlier at this time. We will continue to monitor trends in the\nforecast, especially as more hi-res guidance comes in. \n\nLate this week and into the weekend, zonal to northwest flow will \nset up over the region. We might see some weak waves work through \nthe flow, but much uncertainty remains on moisture availability. \nGuidance has trended drier through the weekend than in previous \nruns, with chances between 15-30% at this time.",
